WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.378
Log injection occurs when untrusted data
changes how a telemetry record is

2
00:00:04.458 --> 00:00:05.196
interpreted.

3
00:00:05.276 --> 00:00:08.784
A username, path, header, or message can
contain carriage returns,

4
00:00:08.864 --> 00:00:13.173
line feeds, control sequences, delimiters,
or text that resembles another event.

5
00:00:13.253 --> 00:00:16.700
When application code builds a plain-text
line by interpolation,

6
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:21.396
attacker-controlled characters may blur
record boundaries or create a forged-looking

7
00:00:21.476 --> 00:00:21.845
entry.

8
00:00:21.925 --> 00:00:23.956
The problem is not limited to newlines;

9
00:00:24.036 --> 00:00:27.606
it is a confusion between data and the
syntax that defines the record.

10
00:00:27.686 --> 00:00:29.533
Prefer structured serialization.

11
00:00:29.613 --> 00:00:33.367
The application supplies a
server-controlled event name and severity,

12
00:00:33.447 --> 00:00:37.202
then places validated or bounded untrusted
values in dedicated fields.

13
00:00:37.282 --> 00:00:41.590
A capable serializer encodes those values
as data rather than asking the handler to

14
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:42.839
construct log syntax.

15
00:00:42.919 --> 00:00:46.920
This gives collectors and query tools
clearer boundaries and avoids brittle

16
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.277
application-wide character replacement.

17
00:00:49.357 --> 00:00:52.250
Limit the length of request-controlled
values as well,

18
00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:56.392
because even correctly encoded data can
consume storage, overload indexing,

19
00:00:56.472 --> 00:00:58.011
or make dashboards unusable.

20
00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:00.861
Structured output does not finish the
security work.

21
00:01:00.941 --> 00:01:03.464
Exporters may convert JSON into delimited
text,

22
00:01:03.544 --> 00:01:05.883
terminals may interpret control sequences,

23
00:01:05.963 --> 00:01:09.225
and dashboards or HTML log viewers may
render fields unsafely.

24
00:01:09.305 --> 00:01:12.998
SIEM ingestion rules can also split,
truncate, or reinterpret values.

25
00:01:13.078 --> 00:01:17.633
Every transformation and presentation
layer must preserve field boundaries and apply

26
00:01:17.713 --> 00:01:19.929
encoding appropriate to its destination.

27
00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:24.009
A CSV export, terminal, and web page have
different rendering requirements.

28
00:01:24.089 --> 00:01:27.105
Event names, field names, and severity
levels are schema,

29
00:01:27.185 --> 00:01:28.970
so keep them under server control.

30
00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:33.174
Do not accept an arbitrary client-provided
log level or use request input as the

31
00:01:33.254 --> 00:01:33.869
event name.

32
00:01:33.949 --> 00:01:38.196
Correlation identifiers and displayed
values should be validated, normalized,

33
00:01:38.276 --> 00:01:42.646
and length-bounded, but avoid claiming
that a short list of replaced characters is a

34
00:01:42.726 --> 00:01:43.834
universal solution.

35
00:01:43.914 --> 00:01:48.161
The durable principle is that untrusted
values belong inside structured fields,

36
00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:51.380
never in the syntax that determines what
event was recorded.
